<p>“It’s a sunny morning in ancient Greece. Students have started gathering in the Academy, but their famous teacher Plato is nowhere to be seen.” The great philosopher has, it seems, “overslept and missed his own lecture.” The sun and the sound of early-rising animals may have failed Plato that day, as the story goes, but never would he let it happen again — at least after he got the mechanism worked out for the first alarm clock in history. Its design involves a series of containers linked by pipes through which water flows at a steady speed all night long. When the water accumulates in the penultimate, sealed vessel, air is pushed out through the flute embedded in its wall, producing something like birdsong.</p>
<p>Plato’s water alarm clock is just one of the pieces of advanced ancient Greek technology explained in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F20zQmyZa20">the new video above from<em> Lost in Time</em></a><em>,</em>&nbsp;a YouTube channel whose <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2025/09/the-advanced-technology-of-ancient-rome.html">look at ancient Roman technology</a> we posted here on Open Culture last year.</p>
