<p>Most of the others were invented for less gentle purposes: we have a nasty-looking flamethrower on wheels meant to breach stone fortresses; a “machine gun” able to fire arrows much more rapidly than any bowman; a nine-story-tall proto-tank fully stocked with catapults and soldiers ready to swarm a city; and a gigantic claw, designed by no less a mind than Archimedes, able to grab and overturn invading warships. Yet as formidable as they sound, evidence of just how all these machines of war worked — and just how well they functioned in actual battle — remains scarce.</p>
<p>Much clearer are records of the <em>Diolkos</em>, recently mentioned here when we featured <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/10/the-megaprojects-even-the-ancient-romans-couldnt-pull-off.html">the megaprojects that even ancient Rome couldn’t pull off</a>. Among those endeavors was a canal across the Isthmus of Corinth, which would save ships having to take the long way around the Peloponnesian peninsula. Or rather, it would save them having to be dragged by a couple hundred slaves over the <em>Diolkos</em>, a paved trackway built in the sixth century BC. Using ramps and winches, ships could be shifted onto a wheeled cart that ran in grooves the whole way, making the whole system a precursor of the railroad, or perhaps an inverted ancestor of&nbsp;the ferry still used to transport railway cars <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2025/03/why-there-isnt-a-bridge-from-italy-to-sicily.html">across the Strait of Messina</a>. Like the Romans, the Greeks also considered digging a canal, but to do so might have violated the intentions of Zeus, a better excuse not to build infrastructure than some of those we hear in the twenty-first century.</p>
